  <img decoding="async" width="768" height="432" src="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/fincen-768x432-1.jpg" class="attachment-medium_large size-medium_large wp-post-image" alt="FinCEN Names Misuse of Cryptocurrencies a National Priority" loading="lazy" style="float:left; margin:0 15px 15px 0;" srcset="https://technologymanias.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/fincen-768x432-1.jpg 768w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/fincen-300x169.jpg 300w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/fincen-1024x576.jpg 1024w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/fincen-696x385.jpg 696w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/fincen-1068x601.jpg 1068w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/fincen-747x420.jpg 747w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/fincen-190x107.jpg 190w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/fincen-380x214.jpg 380w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/fincen-760x428.jpg 760w, https://static.news.bitcoin.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/fincen.jpg 1280w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 768px) 100vw, 768px" /></p> 
  
  <p>
    <strong>The Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN) has named “cybercrime, including relevant cybersecurity and virtual currency considerations” a national priority. In particular, the Treasury is concerned about the “misuse of virtual assets that exploits and undermines their innovative potential, including through laundering of illicit proceeds.”</strong>
  </p>
  
  <p>
    FinCEN, a bureau of the U.S. Department of the Treasury, published its “Anti-Money Laundering and Countering the Financing of Terrorism National Priorities” last week.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    One of the priorities listed is “cybercrime, including relevant cybersecurity and virtual currency considerations.” The document details:
  </p>
  
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Treasury is particularly concerned about cyber-enabled financial crime, ransomware attacks, and the misuse of virtual assets that exploits and undermines their innovative potential, including through laundering of illicit proceeds.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  
  <p>
    The document adds that “Countering ransomware has been identified as a top priority for the United States.” Among the strategies developed to combat ransomware attacks is “enabling rapid tracing and interdiction of virtual currency proceeds,” the document notes.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    <!-- growjs zone placement 31 -->
    
    <ins> <ins class="growjs-placement"></ins> </ins> <!-- end of growjs zone placement -->
  </p>
  
  <p>
    FinCEN wrote that convertible virtual currencies (CVCs) “have grown as the currency of preference in a wide variety of online illicit activity.”
  </p>
  
  <p>
    The bureau detailed: “Criminals use a number of techniques to obscure the source of illicit funds when conducting transactions involving CVCs, including the use of mixers and tumblers.”
  </p>
  
  <p>
    In June, President Joe Biden issued a directive to federal agencies to prioritize efforts to confront global anti-corruption, and cryptocurrency was a focus. The Biden administration also unveiled its strategy to combat ransomware that included expanding cryptocurrency analysis to find criminal transactions.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    <em><strong>What do you think about FinCEN naming the misuse of cryptocurrency a national priority? Let us know in the comments section below.</strong></em>
  </p>
